2026-05-22 00:03:43,465 - INFO - Response: Viruses are tiny infectious agents that can only replicate inside living cells. They consist of genetic material (DNA or RNA) enclosed in a protein coat called a capsid. The capsid is surrounded by a protective envelope made of lipids and proteins.

When a virus enters a host cell, it uses its genetic material to direct the cell's machinery to produce more copies of the virus. This process is called viral replication. Once the virus has replicated enough copies, it can leave the cell and infect other cells, continuing the cycle of infection.

Viruses can cause a wide range of diseases in humans, animals, and plants. Some common examples include the flu, HIV/AIDS, and the COVID-19 virus.
